/E (The chart shows that most of the important information sits in the first few factors. The very first factor carries a lot on its own, and the line drops sharply right after it. The second and third factors still add useful detail, but each factor after them contributes only a small amount. From the fourth factor onward, the curve levels off, so keeping more factors offers little value. In short, relying on just the first three factors should capture the key patterns while keeping the analysis simple.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
